course: dictionary
lesson: 8654
title: Working out seasick from context
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: You can often work out a new word by reading the words around it. These words are its context, and they leave clues.
explain: «The poem after the story says you are at "Fifty North and Forty West" when a ship rolls in a storm and everyone feels seasick.» The ship is rolling up and down in a storm, and everyone feels unwell. So seasick must mean feeling sick because the boat is moving.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence in place of the hard word. «Everyone feels feeling sick because the boat moves» still makes sense, so you have probably got it right.
fact: Seasick means feeling sick, dizzy or wanting to vomit because a boat or ship is moving up and down on the water.
fact: Seasick is an adjective, and it can change to «more seasick».
fact: Seasickness happens because your eyes and your inner ear send your brain different messages about movement.
fact: Tips for seasickness: look at the horizon, get fresh air, and avoid reading.
fact: In stories, a seasick pirate or explorer is often funny.
exercise: «The ferry rocked all morning, and Sam began to feel seasick.» What does seasick mean? | Cold and wet from sea spray | Asleep in a cabin | Feeling very happy on a boat | *Feeling sick because a boat is moving on the water
exercise: Which of these is a tip to stop feeling seasick? | Close your eyes and read a comic | Stay sitting in a dark room with no air | *Look at the horizon | Read a long book
exercise: Which of these is a sign of being seasick? | Feeling warm after a swim | *Feeling dizzy and wanting to be sick | Wanting to dance | Having a loud laugh
test: What is the best meaning of seasick? | Feeling cold on a winter day | Feeling tired after a long walk | *Feeling sick, dizzy or wanting to vomit on a moving boat | Feeling hungry at lunchtime
test: The ship rolled so much in the storm that all the sailors felt ___. | sunburnt | hungry | sleepy | *seasick
exam: Which words in «The poem after the story says you are at "Fifty North and Forty West" when a ship rolls in a storm and everyone feels seasick.» are the best clue for seasick? | the poem after the story | Fifty North and Forty West | *a ship rolls in a storm | everyone feels
